WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.030
The first symbol is a cue, and

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.450
then the line goes quiet.

3
00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.930
A weak memory forgets that

4
00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.110
cue before the end.

5
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.340
Today: how a recurrent net reads one

6
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.960
symbol at a time, and why its memory

7
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:15.360
fades.

8
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.660
A sequence is read one symbol at a time.

9
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.090
The hidden state is the only

10
00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.070
memory the next symbol will see.

11
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.200
Each step keeps only a

12
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.340
share of that state.

13
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.740
If the share is near zero, the cue is

14
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.360
gone after a few empty steps.

15
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.030
Near one, the sign of the cue is

16
00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.210
still there at the end.

17
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.020
That is the limit of this simple net: a

18
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.200
long gap washes the start away.

19
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.670
Here is how much of the cue is left.

20
00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.390
A weak memory keeps almost none, and a

21
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.410
strong memory keeps most of it.

22
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.040
Quick check!

23
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.980
Three empty steps follow a cue,

24
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.020
and the memory is near zero.

25
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.080
What is left at the end?

26
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:04.590
Almost none of the cue.

27
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.460
A small memory, multiplied again and

28
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.680
again, washes the start away.

29
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.060
In the app, open the memory lab.

30
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.100
Find a memory that keeps more than half

31
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.540
the cue, and one that keeps almost none.

32
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:21.560
Your turn.

33
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.500
Does a recurrent net read the

34
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:25.940
whole sequence in one look?

35
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:27.980
Remember this.

36
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.540
The hidden state is the memory,

37
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.980
and a long gap fades it.

38
00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.590
Attention, next, looks back instead.

39
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.260
Try it on paper tonight.

40
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.260
Start from a cue of one, keep

41
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.980
half, and take one empty step.
